

# type(scope)!: imperative summary, lower-case, no full stop (<= 72 chars)
#
# The same structure as the PR description, in miniature. Wrap at 72.
# Every commit, whatever its type, has a body with a Why: paragraph; add
# What changes: and Evidence: where they say something the subject does
# not. A one-line Why: is enough for a small, obvious change.
#
# Why: the problem with the current code, and why this way rather than
# the obvious alternative. Numbers for any performance, size or cost claim.
#
# What changes: what a user or caller sees differently, and what
# deliberately does not change.
#
# Evidence: the command that proves it and its result (`cargo test -p x`,
# 41 passed); a test that fails without this commit.
#
# Trailers, in one final paragraph, one per line, each only when it applies:
#   Refs: #123            (or Closes: #123)
#   Security: the trust-boundary change: who can now do what they could not
#   BREAKING-CHANGE: what breaks and how to migrate (with "!" in the subject).
#                    Upper case: tools ignore "Breaking-Change:".
#   Fixes: <12-char sha> ("subject")   (git show -s --pretty=reference)
#   Co-authored-by: Name <email>
#
# One logical change per commit, and each commit builds. Order a series
# move -> refactor -> test -> behaviour -> regenerate -> docs.
# Fixups: git commit --fixup=<sha>, then git rebase -i --autosquash.
# Review notes, prompts and "addressed comments" belong in the PR, not here.
# Use it: git config commit.template .gitmessage
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------- perch ------
# Types add `deploy`; scopes are crate/package directory names or the list
# in CONTRIBUTING.md. In the changelog (cliff.toml):
#   Wasm: perch-account, perch-account-factory
#        The contracts whose bytes this commit changes. A build/ci/chore/
#        style commit is left out of the changelog unless it has this
#        trailer. Omit it when no bytes change (never "Wasm: none").
#   Security: lands the commit under Security, whatever its type (so does
#        a breaking fix, `fix!:`). Omit it when there is none.
# A pin or dependency move that changes contract bytes can also be
# fix(deps) / feat(deps)!.
